Question 4
For the methodology of the indicator "Sterility" provide materials on verification 
(applicability) of the membrane filtration method in accordance with EAEU Ph. 2.1.6.1;

Answer/Response:
a) Comparative data using validated new methods (m3.2.S.4.2) and late clinical HPLC method (valid from 2009) for impurities are provided in M3.2.S.7.3.11, including additional stability data for primary stability batch 7486-P54/000001 (BXR9ZRT). Samples at initial and after 24 months were analyzed with the new impurity method to demonstrate both methods are stability indicating.

b) Bayer explains primary stability batches were manufactured and are on stability at Esteve Quimica, which uses the late-stage Phase III method; no analytical method transfer occurred to enable testing with new methods at Esteve. Bayer provides updated stability data: two primary stability batches assessed over 36 months and one over 24 months tested with late clinical HPLC methods (valid from 2019) as supportive; and three commercial batches with 18 months long-term stability tested with validated new methods per m3.2.S.4.2. Bayer proposes a retest period of 30 months per ICH Q1E extrapolation.

c) Retest period document revised to state “Store at room temperature (15°C to 30°C)”.

Sources:
M3.2.S.7.1.01_022984560_03 (amended);
M3.2.S.7.1.02_022992153_02 (amended);
M3.2.S.7.3.01_022984561_04 (amended);
M3.2.S.7.3.01_022983162_03 (amended; primary stability);
M3.2.S.7.3.11 (new).

a) Bayer agrees to implement an earlier sampling time point prospectively for commercial DP, but explains Tier II dissolution (for capsule shell crosslinking) uses a 15 min pepsin treatment and first sampling in Tier II can only occur at 20 min due to dispersion steps; therefore Bayer proposes Q=80% at 20 min. Bayer notes ageing effects in gelatin shell may increase Tier II testing frequency and retrospective compilation at 20 min is not possible if previous tests used different sampling time.

b) Bayer proposes tightening Elinzanetant N-oxide release limit to max 0.3% (not 0.2%) citing highest batch release result above reporting threshold of 0.16% and considering analytical and batch-to-batch variability and qualified levels.

c) Bayer proposes to keep shelf life assay at 90.0–105.0%, stating no unambiguous decreasing trend in long-term, but trends under intermediate/accelerated and increase in total degradation products; with release lower limit 95.0% and total degradation products allowed up to 5.0%, 90.0% at end of shelf life is considered justified.

d) Bayer argues cited EMA guideline is applicable only to veterinary medicines since 01 Jan 2008 and Health Canada aqueous solutions guidance is not applicable to a non-aqueous lipid solution softgel. Bayer states no compendial requirements for antioxidants apply and ICH Q6A requirements are not applicable (not oral liquid). Bayer notes alpha-tocopherol has been monitored in stability using validated HPLC, with no meaningful trend beyond analytical variability; justification for not testing is in M3.2.P.5.6.01.

e) Revised release and shelf life specifications in M3.2.P.5.1 are provided.

